**Commit:** `1cdda0d` on `selfheal/r1-orphan-reclaim`.

One deviation you need to weigh before this ships, then the three logs.

## The prescribed identity check does not work on this machine

The brief said identity must come from an environment marker read back with `ps -E -o args= -p <pid>`, "verified on this machine". It is not readable here. I measured it two ways before writing any code:

```
$ WAKELITE_RUN_ID=probe-123 sleep 20 & ps -E -o args= -p $!
sleep 20
```

and directly against the syscall `ps` itself uses, `KERN_PROCARGS2`, on a direct child of the calling process:

```
b'\x02\x00\x00\x00/bin/sleep\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00/bin/sleep\x0020\x00'
HAS MARKER: False
```

macOS 26.5.1 (build 25F80) returns argv only for a non-root caller, even for your own child. `sudo` would show it, but the runner is a user launchd agent. Shipping the check as specified would have classified every orphan as `pid_reused` and left it running — a safety net that never fires, which is worse than none.

So identity is **process start time**: `active_runs` gains a `pid_started_at` column, recorded from `ps -o lstart=` right after `Popen`, and reclaim compares it. A recycled PID has a different start time. `WAKELITE_RUN_ID` / `WAKELITE_TIMER_ID` are still injected into every child and still checked first, so the check tightens by itself if the runner ever runs as root or moves to Linux. Command-line matching stays absent, as you said.

Two consequences worth knowing: rows written before this column existed carry no start time, so the very first restart after deploy cannot reclaim anything (it records `orphan_unverified` and leaves the process alone). And an unjudgeable PID is never signalled — the safe direction.

The port follow-on is in too, opt-in via `resources[].port`. Its ownership proof is an open fd on the timer's own run log, since that survives the `active_runs` row being deleted, which is the case the port path exists for.

⚠️ The `port` key is a schema addition: a rolled-back runner rejects any timer carrying it, so a rollback must be paired with removing `port` from every timer that declares one. Nothing declares one today, so the cost is zero until a timer opts in. This is spelled out in the commit message.

